  <section class="ip-section" aria-labelledby="who-title">
    <div class="ip-section-head">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">Who it is for</div>
      <h2 id="who-title">Built for the people who answer for the code and the bill</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-people">
      <div class="ip-person"><div class="ip-fit-label">Primary</div><h3>CTOs and engineering leads, seed to Series B</h3><p class="ip-why">Lets the team use Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and any model in one place, shows what each session and model costs, and reviews every pull request without a per-seat review tool.</p></div>
      <div class="ip-person"><div class="ip-fit-label">Primary</div><h3>Founding engineers</h3><p class="ip-why">Runs several agents at once on existing subscriptions, delegates to specialists, tests the running app in a real browser and merges only after the project's own checks pass.</p></div>
      <div class="ip-person"><div class="ip-fit-label">Growth</div><h3>Technical solo founders</h3><p class="ip-why">Free to start with free models, and ships from the task: environment variables, Supabase migrations and Vercel deploys.</p></div>
      <div class="ip-person"><div class="ip-fit-label">Growth</div><h3>Agencies and dev shops</h3><p class="ip-why">Keeps each client's model bill separate with their own keys, tracks spend per project, keeps rules in each repo and runs scheduled agents across many repos.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ip-section" aria-labelledby="roadmap-title">
    <div class="ip-section-head">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">Roadmap · October 2026 to March 2027</div>
      <h2 id="roadmap-title">From running agents to building the model behind them</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-road">
      <div class="ip-milestone">
        <span class="ip-stage">Prototype built</span>
        <h3>Computer Use</h3>
        <p>Agents that see and operate desktop apps, not just code and the browser. A native helper, per-app permission grants and input controls are built and in testing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-milestone">
        <span class="ip-stage">Early version live</span>
        <h3>Multiplayer</h3>
        <p>Today a teammate can join a live workspace with one invite link. Next: Vector Teams with shared defaults, roles so guests no longer get full terminal access, and presence across sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-milestone">
        <span class="ip-stage">In development</span>
        <h3>Vector Codium</h3>
        <p>Managed coding models on one Vector account, with no provider keys: monthly plans or top-ups. The plans are priced; payments are not connected yet.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-milestone">
        <span class="ip-stage">In development</span>
        <h3>Vector Velocity</h3>
        <p>Vector's own coding model, planned at about 400B parameters, designed for the loop Vector runs and offered beside every other provider.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="ip-footnote"><strong>Also on the way:</strong> spend caps that stop a run before it crosses a budget (built, in review), signed installers, and one-click sign-in for Vercel and Supabase (waiting on app registration).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ip-split" aria-label="Business model and limitations">
    <div class="ip-panel">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">Business model</div>
      <h2>Free workspace, paid model credits</h2>
      <p class="ip-muted">The workspace stays free, which puts the agents, review and testing in front of every developer. Revenue comes from <strong class="ip-ink">Vector Codium</strong>, a managed service for coding models: one account, no provider keys, the same curated models at every price. <span class="ip-status">In development</span></p>
      <div class="ip-tiers ip-num" aria-label="Plans">
        <span class="ip-tier"><b>$10</b> <span>/ mo</span></span>
        <span class="ip-tier"><b>$50</b> <span>/ mo</span></span>
        <span class="ip-tier"><b>$100</b> <span>/ mo</span></span>
        <span class="ip-tier"><b>$200</b> <span>/ mo</span></span>
      </div>
      <p class="ip-muted ip-small">Monthly plans or one-time top-ups of model credits, with no automatic overages. Teams that bring their own keys keep paying their provider directly.</p>
      <ol class="ip-flywheel">
        <li><b>Free workspace</b><span>Agents, review, testing and cost tracking bring developers in.</span></li>
        <li><b>Codium</b><span>Teams that want one bill and no keys buy model credits.</span></li>
        <li><b>Velocity</b><span>Codium serves Vector's own model beside other labs', so more of that spend stays with Vector.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-panel">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">Limitations today</div>
      <h2>What is not there yet</h2>
      <ul class="ip-clean ip-limits">
        <li><strong>Very early.</strong> First previews shipped in September 2026 and usage analytics started in October 2026, so there is no retention data yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Codex still wins on cost and speed.</strong> In Vector's own matched test (October 2026), Codex used less time, cost and memory per task; work to close the gap is under way.</li>
        <li><strong>Velocity is a plan, not a model yet:</strong> no weights and no benchmarks.</li>
        <li><strong>Unsigned installers.</strong> macOS and Windows show a warning on first launch until code signing is set up.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows and Linux trail macOS</strong> in testing and maturity.</li>
        <li><strong>No SOC 2, SSO or admin console</strong> for larger companies yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Spend caps are built but not released,</strong> and Vercel and Supabase connect with pasted tokens until hosted sign-in is approved.</li>
        <li><strong>Built on open source.</strong> The agent core is adapted from an MIT-licensed open-source coding agent; the workspace, orchestration, cost and review layers are Vector's own.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

